  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 114 PM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nCurrent upper air models show ridge over the Rockies, with\nnorthwest flow over the forecast area. 19Z surface analysis shows\nhigh pressure over western North Dakota this afternoon, while a\nfrontal boundary sits over eastern Wyoming into Nebraska. Winds\nare currently light and variable over the area, with temperatures\nin the 80s. Satellite shows some CU development over the Black\nHills, otherwise mostly clear skies.\n\nAn isolated strong to severe storm cannot be ruled out this\nafternoon and evening. The best chance for storms will be in the\nBlack Hills, as well as northeast Wyoming to southwest SD closer\nto the frontal boundary. CAPE values in the 200-500 j/Kg range\nalong with eff shear 30-40kts and modest lapse rates will support\na small hail and wind threat if any storms are able to get going.\nAnother weak disturbance progged to move through the region\nThursday, bringing more isolated storm chances, mainly over the\nBlack Hills to southwestern SD. Later Thursday into Friday the\nupper ridge moves over the region, prompting warmer temperatures,\nwith highs over the weekend ranging from the upper 80s to near\n100. Sunday into Monday an upper low treks across the northern\nplains and southern Canada, however better forcing looks to remain\nnorth of our area. Cannot rule out some showers and storms on\nSunday, mainly over northwestern SD. \n\nAs the upper low slides eastward, pattern shifts for our region,\nwith southwest flow setting up early next week. This will lower\ntemperatures a bit before shortwave ridging moves back in Tuesday.\nIn typical August fashion, weak disturbances will bring near daily\nchances for isolated showers and storms.",
